<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1682">1682</a>–<a href="/years/1719">1719</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg was a German Lutheran missionary, one of the first Protestant missionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Sent by King Frederick IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, he arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> in 1706 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-plutschau">Heinrich Plütschau</a>, learned Tamil, founded schools and set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press. His Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, printed in 1714, was the first translation of the Bible into an Indian language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartholomaus-ziegenbalg.jpg" alt="Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziegenbalg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ziegenbalg was born in Pulsnitz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, into a devout family; his father was a grain merchant. He studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-halle">University of Halle</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-hermann-francke">August Hermann Francke</a>, then the centre of Pietist Lutheranism. Under Francke's influence King Frederick IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> chose Ziegenbalg and his fellow student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-plutschau">Heinrich Plütschau</a> for a mission to the Danish colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a>, where they landed on 9 July 1706. The plan was for Ziegenbalg to learn Portuguese and Plütschau Tamil, but the roles changed, and Ziegenbalg set out to master Tamil without a grammar to help him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Together the two founded the Danish-Halle Mission and baptised their first Indian converts on 12 May 1707. Their work met opposition from local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and from the Danish authorities. After Ziegenbalg intervened for the widow of a Tamil barber in a debt case, the commander of the Danish fort, Hassius, had him arrested, and he spent a little more than four months in prison. His strained relations with Hassius were one reason for his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1714–1716, during which he married.</p><p>Ziegenbalg began translating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> in 1708 and finished in 1711; his revisions and the need for a Tamil typeface delayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. In 1712 the Society for Promoting Christian Knowledge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> sent the mission a press with type, paper, ink and a printer, making his work one of the first ecumenical ventures of Protestant missions. He went on to the Old Testament and had reached the Book of Ruth at his death. He listed the 161 Tamil books he had read in his Bibliothece Malabarke of 1708, and his press produced perhaps the first almanac printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ill all his life, Ziegenbalg died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> on 23 February 1719, aged thirty-six, and was buried in the New Jerusalem Church he had helped to found there in 1718. His writings on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and Tamil ethics were printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> only 150 to 250 years later; Francke had told him the missionaries' duty was &quot;to extirpate heathenism, and not to spread heathenish nonsense in Europe&quot;. Johann Phillip Fabricius later revised his Tamil New Testament, which soon fell out of use. The historian Stephen Neill judged him too ready to call on the civil power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1706 the German missionaries <b>Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg</b> and Heinrich Plütschau, sent by the Danish king, founded the Tranquebar Mission and translated the New Testament into Tamil.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-india">Danish India</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pulsnitz, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tharangambadi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.18,14.01&amp;year=1706" aria-label="Pulsnitz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4868 390 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bartholomäus Ziegenbalg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5318 660h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5318 660h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.18,14.01&amp;year=1706">What was here in 1706? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
